---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Privacy · ${site.name}`}
  description="Privacy policy for Hermes Agent, a private single-user application used by the owner of this site to work with his own Google Account."
>
  <article class="prose privacy">
    <header>
      <p class="label">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="meta">Applies to Hermes Agent · Effective 2026-09-18</p>
    </header>

    <p>
      Hermes Agent is a private, single-user software application. It runs on hardware owned and
      operated by Joshua Ford, the owner of this site, and it is used by him and no one else. It is
      not offered to the public, it has no sign-up, and it has no other accounts. This page
      explains what the application can access in a Google Account, what happens to that
      information, and how access can be removed.
    </p>

    <h2>What the application can access</h2>

    <p>
      With the account owner's explicit consent, Google grants the application access to the
      following services. Each one is used only to answer a request the owner has made of his own
      assistant, in the same way a person reads their own mail and calendar to get something done.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Gmail</strong> · Reading messages, and composing or sending mail the owner asks for.</li>
      <li><strong>Google Calendar</strong> · Reading, creating, changing, or removing events on the owner's own calendars.</li>
      <li><strong>Google Drive</strong> · Finding and reading files in the owner's own Drive.</li>
      <li><strong>Google Docs</strong> · Reading and editing documents in the owner's own Drive.</li>
      <li><strong>Google Sheets</strong> · Reading and editing spreadsheets in the owner's own Drive.</li>
      <li><strong>Contacts</strong> · Reading the owner's own contacts to resolve names and addresses.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The application is not able to access the Google Accounts of any other person. It holds no
      data belonging to anyone but the account owner.
    </p>

    <h2>How the information is used</h2>

    <p>
      Information from these services is used for exactly one purpose: to carry out tasks the owner
      requests of his own assistant, such as finding a message, checking a calendar, or reading a
      document. It is never used for advertising, profiling, or any purpose unrelated to those
      requests.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the information goes</h2>

    <p>
      The assistant runs on hardware in the owner's home, and its records stay there. To interpret a
      request and draft a response, the relevant excerpt from a Google service may be sent to a
      third-party language model provider for processing, which is governed by that provider's own
      terms. No Google user data is sold, rented, or shared with any other party, and none of it is
      used to train machine learning models at the owner's direction.
    </p>

    <p>
      This site and this application use no analytics, no cookies, no advertising, and no tracking
      of any kind.
    </p>

    <h2>Google API Services User Data Policy</h2>

    <p>
      Hermes Agent's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs to any other
      application will adhere to the
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy" rel="noopener">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
      including the Limited Use requirements. In practice that means the data is used only to
      provide the features described above, is never transferred except as needed to provide or
      improve those features or as required by law, is never used for advertising, and is never read
      by humans except with the owner's consent, for security purposes, or to comply with the law.
    </p>

    <h2>Storage and retention</h2>

    <p>
      Authorization credentials are stored on the owner's own machine with file permissions that
      restrict them to the owner. There is no server operated by anyone else that holds a copy of
      this data. Exchanges with the assistant are kept in its local history on that machine, the
      same way a person keeps their own notes, and the owner can delete that history at any time.
    </p>

    <h2>Removing access</h2>

    <p>
      Access can be revoked at any time, with immediate effect, from the Google Account's
      <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections" rel="noopener">third-party connections page</a>.
      Revoking access stops all further use. It does not delete the owner's own Google data, which
      remains exactly as it was and is not held anywhere else.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>

    <p>
      Questions about this policy, or about anything above, can go to
      <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>

    <p>
      If this policy changes, the effective date at the top of this page changes with it. The
      current version is always the one published here.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .privacy header {
    padding-block: var(--space-8) var(--space-2);
  }

  .privacy {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }

  .privacy h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
  }
</style>
